article_page('hindu-daughter-inheritance-rights-after-conversion', <<<'HTML'
<p>India has several personal laws that govern family matters, including inheritance. A question that often comes up is whether a Hindu daughter loses her right to inherit her parents' property if she converts to another religion.</p>
<h2>The legal position</h2>
<p>Under the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, daughters have the same rights as sons to inherit their parents' property. Some families believe that a daughter who converts to another religion, such as Islam or Christianity, automatically loses those rights.</p>
<p>That belief comes from custom rather than from the text of the law. <strong>The Act does not disqualify a person from inheriting merely because they have converted.</strong> Courts have upheld a converted daughter's right to inherit from her Hindu parents.</p>
<h2>Key points</h2>
<h3>1. Inheritance is a legal right, not a religious one</h3>
<p>A daughter's right to inherit is based on her relationship to the deceased, not on her religious identity at the time of inheritance.</p>
<h3>2. Conversion does not make a daughter an outsider</h3>
<p>She remains part of her natal family. Unless her parent has made a valid will leaving the property to others, she continues to have the same rights as the other heirs.</p>
<h3>3. Protection against discrimination</h3>
<p>This position prevents customs from being used to deny women their rightful share, and is consistent with the constitutional rights to equality and freedom of religion.</p>
<h3>4. The exception: descendants of a convert</h3>
<p>Section 26 of the Act provides that the <strong>children and descendants born to a convert after conversion</strong> are disqualified from inheriting from their Hindu relatives, unless they are Hindus when the succession opens. The convert herself is not disqualified.</p>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>A Hindu daughter does not lose her legal right to inherit her parents' property just because she embraces a different religion. Family bonds and legal entitlements are decided by law and fairness, not by personal faith.</p>
